    "represents": "Choosing an RF frequency to separate bunches",
    "remarks": [
      {
        "kind": "kept",
        "line": "The phase difference is ωΔt",
        "detail": "Two bunches Δt apart meet the field at phases ωΔt apart; they are kicked oppositely near half a period."
      },
      {
        "kind": "simplified",
        "line": "Antennas are point dipoles",
        "detail": "Each antenna is an oscillating point dipole with its exact retarded fields (near, induction and radiation zones); real RF deflectors are resonant cavities or plates driven by a generator."
      },
      {
        "kind": "simplified",
        "line": "Light is slow here",
        "detail": "c is a few cells per time unit, so that the board spans less than a wavelength of the RF and the particle crosses it in a few periods; the physics depends only on such ratios (v/c, the wavelength against the board), which real devices share."
      }
    ]
  },
